A1. A sign of celebrity. ~ AUTOGRAPH
A9. The last word in aeroplanes. ~ JUMP
A12. What every girl in Hollywood wants to be. ~ STARRED
A13. What he did in order to stand well in the community. ~ AROSE
A16. The way he looked after heaping coals of fire on his head. ~ SINGED
A17. In the Wheels of Government, this must be the Speaker of the House. ~ SPOKE
A18. To live in the same old way. ~ BE
A19. A high station in the busy life of Chicago. ~ EL
A20. A small boy's expletive. ~ AW
A21. What Sandy had when the collection box came around. ~ LAPSE
A22. Good Grief! Someone said "This is me." ~ WOE
A23. A directing board. ~ SIGN
A25. A supernatural being in Mohammedan belief. ~ GENIE
A26. The first thing Adam planted in the Garden of Eden. ~ FOOT
A27. A pen house for snooty parties. ~ STY
A28. A rough kid. ~ SUEDE
A29. "On the Sidewalks of New York" (in early spring). ~ SLUSH
A30. Any jay can get joy out of this. ~ OY
A31. You could write volumes on this. ~ DESK
A32. What a Scotchman is apt to do after he signs a check. ~ REPENT
A34. This wood be fuelish. ~ PEAT
A35. He was this in old Kentucky and toasted in Washington. ~ BRED
A37. Headquarters. ~ PILLOW
A39. A good thing to take for seasickness. ~ BOAT
A40. This is now being quoted at 110. ~ CX
A41. The main support of a window-washer (plur.) ~ SILLS
A42. A day when there were no hits, no runs, and no errors. ~ RAINY
A44. A woman knows how to handle this souse. ~ MOP
A45. Presses a suit. ~ WOOS
A46. "Mr. and Mrs. is the name." ~ MARRY
A47. If you want to make a hit, try this on ice. ~ FALL
A48. Where friend wife likes to eat. ~ OUT
A49. Fed up. ~ DINED
A50. This looks like the beginning of trouble. ~ TR
A51. A half a loaf. ~ LO
A52. This bone is all that was left of Oscar when the car was destroyed. ~ OS
A53. These are picking up. ~ TONGS
A54. The way one feels after purchasing a new car. ~ POORER
A56. This one has been on the rack ever since the Democrats have been in power. ~ TOWEL
A57. What red caps go for. ~ BAGGAGE
A58. A high light in Parisian night life. ~ MOON
A59. These are always on the blink. ~ EYELASHES

D1. This gives an added value to your property. ~ ASSESSOR
D2. Beauty is in the garden, but this is in the wheelbarrow. ~ UTILITY
D3. A coat of arms. ~ TAN
D4. An instrumental factor in the church ~ ORGAN
D5. What happened to the tale of the fish that got away. ~ GREW
D6. This will stop you! ~ RED
D7. A little advertisement. ~ AD
D8. The way tired feet feel when parked in old slippers. ~ HAPPIER
D9. Radio chestnut. ~ JOKE
D10. What they got out of their old car. ~ USE
D11. One of us. ~ ME
D14. Wild and Irish. ~ ROSE
D15. These are put in sandwiches. ~ TEETH
D17. Spinach seasoning. ~ SAND
D18. What prosperity will do to prices. ~ BOOST
D21. Anyone who knows his onions should know this. ~ LEEK
D22. This should be well dressed. ~ WOUND
D24. The end of poor Peggy. ~ GY
D25. What Little Willie eats ice-cream with. ~ GUSTO
D26. You can always find this in the Atlantic & Pacific. ~ FLEET
D28. Impressions made by the law. ~ SEALS
D29. This is even finer than a stream-line. ~ SPRAY
D31. Where the farmer who took a wife lived (plur.) ~ DELLS
D33. This should be put on the black list. ~ EBONY
D34. A fellow who's always up in the air. ~ PILOT
D36. What a mosquito does before he bites. ~ EXPLORES
D37. Goody-goody. ~ PIOUS
D38. What some families have over their radio. ~ WRANGLE
D39. Bronx cheer. ~ BIRD
D40. When you get into this you will have to get out by degrees. ~ COLLEGE
D41. You may get a faint idea from this one. ~ SWOON
D43. Cockney rabbits. ~ ARES
D44. An exponent of home-rule. ~ MA
D46. This takes a lot of digging. ~ MINE
D47. Fifty thousand Frenchmen. ~ FROGS
D49. Pretty soft! Pretty soft! ~ DOWN
D50. A habit of Caesars. ~ TOGA
D53. If you get the other words, you can get this also. ~ TOO
D54. What the spiflicated gentleman thinks the whole world is. ~ PAL
D55. A collegiate repeater. ~ RAH
D56. Legally speaking this is a kind of a wit. ~ TO
D57. Add gin to this and it will give you a start. ~ BE


